# Caddy in front of the gateway on the EC2 host (docs/RUNBOOK.md).
#
# What this file must keep true: a long-lived SSE stream is never cut or
# held back by the proxy. The resumable-stream claim is only as good as the
# hop in front of it.
#   - No `encode` directive. Compression buffers; nothing here enables it.
#   - `flush_interval -1` flushes every write to the client immediately,
#     instead of relying on Caddy's content-type sniffing for text/event-stream.
#   - No write timeout (Caddy's default is none) and no response-header
#     timeout, so a stream that is quiet between events is not closed.
#   - `idle` only bounds a *keep-alive connection with no request in flight*;
#     it never cuts an active stream. 10m is generous on purpose.
#
# CADDY_SITE is the public hostname in production (Caddy then obtains and
# renews a Let's Encrypt certificate by itself, over ports 80/443) and
# `:8080` in the local verification stack (plain HTTP, no certificate).
{
	servers {
		timeouts {
			read_header 30s
			idle 10m
		}
	}
}

{$CADDY_SITE} {
	reverse_proxy gateway:3000 {
		flush_interval -1
		transport http {
			dial_timeout 5s
		}
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}
}
